import FormalSystem.Metalogic.Decidability.DecisionProcedure
import BimodalLib.Conjecture

/-!
# Conjectures: the non-admitted outcomes of the enumerated pipeline

The tableau pipeline admits a formula as a theorem entry only when it is valid *and* a
`DerivationTree` was extracted for it. Every other outcome is registered here as a conjecture,
with its verdict, and is never a result.

## One constant per cell, not per formula

A cell is a triple (fragment, frame class, verdict), and there is one `Conjecture` constant per
cell. The alternative — one constant per enumerated formula — is what the count forbids: the
conjecture half of a ruled-bound run is 2,895 formulas, each of which would need a docstring that
`lake exe runLinter BimodalLib` requires and an `INDEX.md` row that `scripts/library-index.sh`
generates. The count here is instead (fragments x frame classes x verdicts) = 4 x 4 x 3 = 48,
bounded independently of how large the conjecture half is. The per-formula fact lives where it
belongs: in the ADMIT record's own `disposition` and `conjecture_verdict` fields, in
`data/theorem-library-pipeline.jsonl`.

## The three verdicts, and why they are three and not two

`DecisionResult` has four constructors and the pipeline keeps all four names. Three of them are
non-admitting:

* `invalid` — the decision procedure returned a countermodel. A cell records the **ungated**
  ones: those whose open branch did not pass all eight hypotheses of `not_valid_of_hasOpen_int`,
  so the verdict is the procedure's own and no theorem stands behind it. A gated one is not a
  conjecture at all; it is a certified refutation, written to
  `data/theorem-library-countermodels.jsonl`. Status: `heuristicallyRefuted`.
* `fuelExhausted` — the tableau ran out of fuel, so validity is genuinely undetermined. The fuel
  landscape is bimodal (a formula resolves at 500 or not at all), so this is not a budget that
  more fuel would settle. Status: `undetermined`.
* `extractionFailed` — **the tableau closed, so the formula IS valid**, but no `|- phi` witness
  was reconstructed. It is never admissible, because the registry's `proof` field needs a witness
  and there is none; and it is not a failure or a timeout, which is why it keeps a cell of its own
  rather than being folded into `fuelExhausted`. Its conjecture runs the other way from the other
  two: it asserts the formula *is* derivable. Status: `undetermined`.

## What each statement says

For `invalid` and `fuelExhausted`: every formula of the cell is **not** derivable. For
`extractionFailed`: every formula of the cell **is** derivable. The cell is defined in the
statement itself, by the fragment the computed profile files the formula in, the complexity bound,
and the verdict `decideAuto` returns — so the statement is checkable against the same functions
the pipeline ran, and not merely a description of a dataset.

## Measured coverage, and where it is partial

At the ruled bound over a two-atom pool: `Boolean` 14 of 14 formulas decided, `ModalBoolean` 133
of 133, `TemporalBoolean` 3,384 of 3,384, and `ModalTemporalBoolean` **424 of 1,688** — that last
fragment was truncated by the run's per-fragment wall-clock budget, so its cells are a lower
bound rather than an exhaustive count. Every note below states its own cell's count and says
whether it is exhaustive.

Two cells are empty by construction rather than by measurement. `fuelExhausted` and
`extractionFailed` are verdicts of the decision procedure, which the pipeline runs at `Base`
only, because proof extraction is `Base`-only: `extractProof` ignores its frame-class argument
and `ProofExtractionResult.success` is hard-coded to `Base`. The cells of those two verdicts at
`Dense`, `ZTime` and `RTime` are therefore **not measured at all**, and their notes say so
instead of reporting a zero as if it were a finding.

## References

* `records/theorem-library.md` — the ADMISSION rule these cells are the complement of
* `data/theorem-library-pipeline.jsonl` — the per-formula records, regenerated by
  `scripts/theorem-library-pipeline.sh`
* `BimodalLib/Theorems/Enumerated/Base.lean` — the admitted half
-/

